WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:09.820
64. Concrete's Glowing Future We can all agree that concrete buildings are

2
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:15.780
almost universally ugly. Concrete may be a cheap and convenient building material, but

3
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:21.540
it's not going to win any beauty contests. That, however, could be about to change. A

4
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:28.260
Mexican scientist named Jose Carlos Rubio Avalos has invented a type of concrete that,

5
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:34.780
amazingly, glows in the dark. Avalos accomplished this by inserting cells that absorb light

6
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:41.060
into cement, a key ingredient in concrete. By day the concrete absorbs light, and when

7
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:47.260
the sun goes down, it begins to glow. It's said to work even on cloudy days. Currently

8
00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.180
Avalos's concrete can emit blue or green light, but he's working on versions that

9
00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:58.700
will glow white, red, and even purple. Avalos's invention has city governments and architects

10
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:05.220
very excited. Having glowing buildings could save cities millions of dollars on electricity,

11
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.660
and architects will have a whole new visual tool to experiment with. Can you imagine how

12
00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:16.860
beautiful the buildings of the future might be, covered in glowing rainbow patterns? There

13
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:23.180
is however one problem, the cost. Glow in the dark concrete is currently five times

14
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:28.980
more expensive to produce than regular concrete. But there is no need to build an entire building

15
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.220
out of glowing concrete. Instead, existing structures could be coated with a thin layer

16
00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:39.340
of Avalos's special material. This would make creating a glow in the dark building

17
00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:45.100
a lot cheaper. But how often, you might ask, would you have to reapply the concrete? Would

18
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:50.820
it be worth the cost and the effort? Fear not. According to Avalos, his concrete can

19
00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:57.580
retain its glowing effect for around a hundred years. That's free light for a century. Thanks

20
00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:02.500
to Jose Avalos, the future of concrete looks very bright indeed.
